# Conditional Rendering



**Topic:** conditional rendering strategies
**You should already know:** components, JSX, props

---

## 1. Real screens change with the data

| Case | What you want |
|---|---|
| A topper badge | Show a medal only when `cgpa` is above 9 |
| Pass or fail | Show one word or the other, never both |
| An empty list | Show "No students found" instead of a blank space |

**The problem:** you cannot write an `if` statement inside JSX. So how do you make a decision there?

---

## 2. Braces take a value, not a statement

```jsx
// ✗ does not work
<div>
  {if (cgpa > 9) { ... }}
</div>

// ✓ an expression works
<div>
  {cgpa > 9 ? "Topper" : ""}
</div>
```

The test from the JSX lecture still applies:

> **If you could put it on the right of an `=` sign, it works inside braces.**

An `if` statement cannot go there. A ternary can, because it produces a value.

---

## 3. Way 1 — the ternary

```
{ condition ? showThis : showThat }
```

```jsx
<p>Result: {cgpa >= 5 ? "Pass" : "Fail"}</p>
```

Read it aloud as: *if this, then that, otherwise the other.*

**Exactly one of the two always appears.**

**Use it when** there are two possibilities and one of them must always show.

---

## 4. A ternary can return JSX too

```jsx
function StudentCard({ name, cgpa }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      {cgpa >= 9
        ? <span>🏅 Topper</span>
        : <span>Keep going</span>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

The two results are **JSX**, not just text. Anything you could return normally can go on either side.

---

## 5. Way 2 — the `&&` pattern

```
{ condition && showThis }
```

```jsx
{cgpa >= 9 && <span>🏅 Topper</span>}
```

- condition is **true** → the badge appears
- condition is **false** → nothing is added at all

**Use it when** there is nothing sensible to show in the other case. This is the more common of the two in real code.

---

## 6. Which one do I use?

| | Ternary `? :` | And `&&` |
|---|---|---|
| Number of outcomes | two | one or nothing |
| Reads as | this **or** that | **only if** |
| Good for | Pass / Fail | a badge, a warning |

> **Simple test:** is there something to show in the other case? Yes → ternary. No → `&&`.

---

## 7. ⚠ The `&&` number trap

```jsx
// ✗ prints 0 on the screen
{marks.length && <List />}

// ✓ compare properly
{marks.length > 0 && <List />}
```

**Why:** `0` is falsy, so `&&` stops and hands back `0` — and React happily prints the number `0` on your page. You get a stray zero floating in your layout with no error message.

**Rule:** always compare, so the condition is a real `true` or `false`.

The same trap applies to empty strings and to `NaN`.

---

## 8. Way 3 — empty states

```jsx
function StudentList({ students }) {
  if (students.length === 0) {
    return <p>No students found.</p>;
  }

  return <div>...the list...</div>;
}
```

**An `if` statement IS allowed here.** This `if` is in the **function body**, above the `return` — not inside braces in JSX. That is perfectly fine.

This is called an **early return**, and it is used constantly in real code. It keeps the main return clean.

**Never leave the screen blank.** A user who sees nothing cannot tell whether the app is loading, broken, or genuinely empty.

---

## 9. Type this in

```jsx
function StudentCard({ name, cgpa, isPlaced }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>{cgpa >= 5 ? "Pass" : "Fail"}</p>
      {cgpa >= 9 && <span>🏅 Topper</span>}
      {isPlaced && <span>✅ Placed</span>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Change the `cgpa` values and watch parts appear and disappear. Send `isPlaced={false}` for one card and see the tick vanish.

